/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f82fa702c211b046081b0fcaee8c5a347d50545af66c632d5e643818e7988a1" "basis" 1023 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f82fa702c211b046081b0fcaee8c5a347d50545af66c632d5e643818e7988a1" "basis" 1023 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-1), (-3,5),$ and $(4,-3).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n\n- $(-5,3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/example-5458.json" "file_sha256" "61938044688b7fc932764702198ebe6a6a6d2cd660696f668f9734838de81e0f" "basis" 1023 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,1), (3,-5), (-4,3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/example-5458.json" "file_sha256" "61938044688b7fc932764702198ebe6a6a6d2cd660696f668f9734838de81e0f" "basis" 1023 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/a43a1658a16151c0306702959c50e8d0288077983ad50a084b39703e206469c0.png)\n\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ shown above. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/example-7186.json" "file_sha256" "480e292c46ad0d9bd280e35cb7f315305674491365dbe0403592b82c66b9390a" "basis" 1023 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/414cb72ff004fe4c5898a0e2c7b60b4b3651473e776388fad21efd8fc9ae6165.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $A\\mathbf{v},$ we need to rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/b24d8d460b7833a49f2bd7183f0f4b5d22d94a7d3b1d3bb9eb51beff9eeb7ef6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/example-7186.json" "file_sha256" "480e292c46ad0d9bd280e35cb7f315305674491365dbe0403592b82c66b9390a" "basis" 1023 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-5,-1), (1,4), (3,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,-5)$\n\n- $(4,-1)$\n\n- $(-5,-3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/example-5457.json" "file_sha256" "23ae715fa99013cbdce0d1c2f6ca84550bc2b34f1788a319a5d06af8a8d4845f" "basis" 1023 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The clockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $270^\\circ.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-1,5), (4,-1), (-5,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/example-5457.json" "file_sha256" "23ae715fa99013cbdce0d1c2f6ca84550bc2b34f1788a319a5d06af8a8d4845f" "basis" 1023 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/c32886c161e347daa9bf6fa7215f8a09abc4334632c33020c31ade6dfc4e7315.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "f07b529fcc489010236892ec02a2f855802235483748b56921c87bff17bae15d" "basis" 1083 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/9fae3ec0ada3008937d88e4e76374a56da4369b1821cfee072c90d88b562df46.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/ed1a5e8a05ee0d9994d8ba90c02463fbf96525b1cd851bec685a005a34834f81.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "f07b529fcc489010236892ec02a2f855802235483748b56921c87bff17bae15d" "basis" 1083 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha