" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5c4668c5142ccaa1e834ae87585f527664d5ca4359d1a4186d1b99f9a76dd80" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-1), (-3,5),$ and $(4,-3).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n\n- $(-5,3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/example-5458.json" "file_sha256" "61938044688b7fc932764702198ebe6a6a6d2cd660696f668f9734838de81e0f" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,1), (3,-5), (-4,3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/example-5458.json" "file_sha256" "61938044688b7fc932764702198ebe6a6a6d2cd660696f668f9734838de81e0f" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/a43a1658a16151c0306702959c50e8d0288077983ad50a084b39703e206469c0.png)\n\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ shown above. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/example-7186.json" "file_sha256" "fad4afe530a5aab6c4b47c32aac6c8b422a56d8809902241cfadb81c58834ebd" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/414cb72ff004fe4c5898a0e2c7b60b4b3651473e776388fad21efd8fc9ae6165.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $A\\mathbf{v},$ we need to rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/b24d8d460b7833a49f2bd7183f0f4b5d22d94a7d3b1d3bb9eb51beff9eeb7ef6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/example-7186.json" "file_sha256" "fad4afe530a5aab6c4b47c32aac6c8b422a56d8809902241cfadb81c58834ebd" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-5,-1), (1,4), (3,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,-5)$\n\n- $(4,-1)$\n\n- $(-5,-3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/example-5457.json" "file_sha256" "23ae715fa99013cbdce0d1c2f6ca84550bc2b34f1788a319a5d06af8a8d4845f" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The clockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $270^\\circ.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-1,5), (4,-1), (-5,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/example-5457.json" "file_sha256" "23ae715fa99013cbdce0d1c2f6ca84550bc2b34f1788a319a5d06af8a8d4845f" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/assets/0c2f0240b962701c2eb2ee34a0eddc164afb8e1a677a5ed7f81fe65efb890358.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "43abc70b10a4fe02227840dde61ae3b78b7af0070de16c757c97eabc632fa06c" "basis" 1944 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/assets/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/assets/578b3315028bf5a19a4b6e17d228db08b41f24dee63e5cb7f8d601ce667785f4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "43abc70b10a4fe02227840dde61ae3b78b7af0070de16c757c97eabc632fa06c" "basis" 1944 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba09fe4f8b3e8f987b40d1982b4556ee333291d5b16043218e78d30bba6e7ace" "basis" 1944 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81823" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "43abc70b10a4fe02227840dde61ae3b78b7af0070de16c757c97eabc632fa06c" "basis" 1944 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-81816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-3,0),(1,5),(2,-2).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(0,3)$\n- $(-1,-5)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-81816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(3,0),(-1,-5),(-2,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-81816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fc8467179c60275ed5867781fe1604e939ace2f02fc93aa0f5e9b62b9d5ad16"} {"question" "q-81816" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-81816" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-81816" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,-3),(2,-2),(1,3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(-1,-3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,3),(-2,2),(-1,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fc8467179c60275ed5867781fe1604e939ace2f02fc93aa0f5e9b62b9d5ad16"} {"question" "q-160374" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160374" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160374" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,1),(-1,2),(1,2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(-1,3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(0,-1),(1,-2),(-1,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fc8467179c60275ed5867781fe1604e939ace2f02fc93aa0f5e9b62b9d5ad16"} {"question" "q-160617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-8396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,1),(3,3),(4,4).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-2,-1)$\n- $(-3,-2)$\n- $(-3,-3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-8396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-2,-1),(-3,-3),(-4,-4).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-8396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fc8467179c60275ed5867781fe1604e939ace2f02fc93aa0f5e9b62b9d5ad16"} {"question" "q-8396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-8396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-8396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,0),(-1,0),(2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(-1,0)$\n- $(2,1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,0),(-1,0),(-2,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fc8467179c60275ed5867781fe1604e939ace2f02fc93aa0f5e9b62b9d5ad16"} {"question" "q-160368" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160368" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160368" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/assets/c32886c161e347daa9bf6fa7215f8a09abc4334632c33020c31ade6dfc4e7315.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/assets/9fae3ec0ada3008937d88e4e76374a56da4369b1821cfee072c90d88b562df46.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/assets/ed1a5e8a05ee0d9994d8ba90c02463fbf96525b1cd851bec685a005a34834f81.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fc8467179c60275ed5867781fe1604e939ace2f02fc93aa0f5e9b62b9d5ad16"} {"question" "q-160624" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1