ar/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/assets/77936c4115b37d79ae301a93b218ecd23114ae4ad42d8298b8f058c1f50e82a2.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians **clockwise** about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ **clockwise** by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/assets/57391cd64fc1c88ef4ee621edeaa84f4111b1b8c6a279f7301f937460cc1b341.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fc8467179c60275ed5867781fe1604e939ace2f02fc93aa0f5e9b62b9d5ad16"} {"question" "q-160622" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160622" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/assets/23f63717c8d356c698137359824112511b0bbd5ec04c59e47d22500d842d03b6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/assets/5a1923eec2b66ef4eb861ec42b00ea48a3285aaaf3255c42d12c001c9cde32f8.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/assets/9038b103842cd92233490de2306c804195675337f28011d8b93434f4f2813279.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/assets/b2beaf70e860c2e94f271cfb09b72aa3a256f0ec267ea85318329dd61ce9f44b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/assets/cbcb59ec78c02f46704f7d56421e4265bb1fa199ecdef6c798ddd4e5fd4349d8.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160622" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/assets/23f63717c8d356c698137359824112511b0bbd5ec04c59e47d22500d842d03b6.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,1),(-1,0),(-1,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(0,-1)$\n- $(-2,1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,0),(0,1),(-2,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fc8467179c60275ed5867781fe1604e939ace2f02fc93aa0f5e9b62b9d5ad16"} {"question" "q-160643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-8395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(0,2),(3,0),(0,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(0,-3)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(2,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-8395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${270}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(2,0),(0,-3),(0,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-8395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fc8467179c60275ed5867781fe1604e939ace2f02fc93aa0f5e9b62b9d5ad16"} {"question" "q-8395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-8395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-8395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-11961" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-3),(0,5),(2,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(3,-2)$\n- $(-5,0)$\n- $(5,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-11961" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(3,-2),(-5,0),(5,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-11961" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fc8467179c60275ed5867781fe1604e939ace2f02fc93aa0f5e9b62b9d5ad16"} {"question" "q-11961" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-11961" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-11961" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-3),(3,1),(-4,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(3,2)$\n- $(1,-3)$\n- $(-1,4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-3,-2),(1,-3),(-1,4).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fc8467179c60275ed5867781fe1604e939ace2f02fc93aa0f5e9b62b9d5ad16"} {"question" "q-160644" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160644" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160644" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-3,1),(1,-4),(1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-1,3)$\n- $(4,1)$\n- $(0,-1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-1,-3),(4,1),(0,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fc8467179c60275ed5867781fe1604e939ace2f02fc93aa0f5e9b62b9d5ad16"} {"question" "q-160645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "q-160645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/state.json" "file_sha256" "c78a64bff1e49bec2822216e061868a70e03b4fecd42ac3e19bac7686e45893c"} {"question" "e-5458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-1), (-3,5),$ and $(4,-3).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n\n- $(-5,3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/example-5458.json" "file_sha256" "c100d4a333fc086d8200170bddea14671099cca4d83bf04a5e4f47a4f8c3f350"} {"question" "e-5458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060869/assets/c7bcce3733cf573fc10315d8dc0e44faa7c4b4f6bcf0274e69d11dafd4055a2e.png)\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,1), (3,-5), (-4,3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Deve